1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. Bc4 d6 5. O-O h6 6. d4 Ne7 7. Nc3 Bg7 8. e5 d5
9. Bb3 c6 10. Ne2 Ng6 11. g3 g4 12. Nxf4 Nxf4 13. Bxf4 gxf3 14. Qxf3 Be6 15. c3
Nd7 16. Bc2 Nf8 17. Qh5 Qd7 18. Bd2 Bg4 19. Qh4 Ng6 20. Bxg6 fxg6 21. e6 Bxe6
22. Rae1 g5 23. Qg4 O-O-O 24. Rxe6 h5 25. Qe2 h4 26. Bxg5 Rdg8 27. Re7 Qh3 28.
g4 Bxd4+ 29. cxd4 Rxg5 30. Re8+ Rxe8 31. Qxe8+ Kc7 32. Qe7+ {Source: Fox and
James' website 2004. Brian Denman's note (dated 20 Feb 2019): "The game
between Aleister Crowley and Shoosmith was originally published in the
Eastbourne Gazette of 4.4.1894. The column was discovered by Chris Ravilious
about twenty years ago. At that time Crowley was very young for a chess
columnist and he used the pseudonym of 'Ta Dhuibh'. Although he only wrote the
articles for about six months, he caused a disagreement within the Eastbourne
CC. If you look at the article, you will see that the names of the players are
not given. It was in fact Crowley's usual practice not to name the players
involved in games and some of these have definitely been traced back to
Crowley. I myself think it likely that this was the Crowley v Shoosmith game,
but there is no clear proof of this and I believe that, when the game is
quoted, a note should be added to say that there is an uncertainty about who
was playing the game."} 1-0

