1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Nbd7 5. e3 c6 6. Nf3 Qa5 7. Bxf6 Nxf6 8.
Nd2 Bb4 9. Qc2 O-O 10. Be2 Ne4 11. Ndxe4 dxe4 12. O-O f5 13. f3 exf3 14. Rxf3
Bd7 15. Rd1 Rae8 16. Kh1 Re7 17. e4 Bxc3 18. bxc3 c5 19. Rdf1 Rfe8 20. Bd3 g6
21. h3 Ba4 22. Qf2 Qxc3 23. exf5 exf5 24. Bxf5 $5 {In a slightly shaky
position, White decides to risk all on a kingside assault. It is not sound but
it works.} Qxd4 $1 ({Not} 24... gxf5 $2 25. Rxf5 {which soon leads to mate.})
25. Qg3 Qe5 26. Qh4 {Again White goes for broke, rather than limp along a pawn
down in an ending.} gxf5 27. Rxf5 Qc7 $2 {[%mdl 32768]} (27... Qd6 $1 {would
have won for Black with best play, though he would still have had to find a
few good moves. He must have overlooked White's very neat finish.}) 28. Qh6 $1
Rg7 29. Qe6+ Rf7 30. Rg5+ {Sources: American Chess Bulletin of August 1904 
(courtesy J S Hilbert), 'Chess Tournaments and Matches 1904' - A J Gillam -
the Chess Player (1999), and the Big Database 2003.} (30. Rg5+ Kf8 31. Qh6+ Ke7
32. Rxf7+ Kxf7 33. Rg7+ {and mate in a futher two moves.}) 1-0

