1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. d3 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Be3 Bb6 7. Qd2 Be6 8. Bb5
Ba5 9. h3 O-O 10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. O-O h6 12. Qe2 c5 13. Bd2 c6 14. Na4 Rb8 15. b3
Bxd2 16. Nxd2 g5 17. Nb2 Kh7 18. Nbc4 Rg8 19. g4 h5 20. f3 Ne8 21. Rf2 f6 22.
Ne3 h4 23. c4 Rg7 24. Nb1 Qa5 25. Qd2 Qa6 26. Nc3 $2 Rxb3 27. Rc1 Rb8 28. Ned1
Rgb7 29. Rc2 Nc7 30. Ne2 Rb1 31. Kg2 Bg8 32. Ndc3 R1b7 33. Rf1 Ne6 34. Nd1 Qa3
35. Ne3 Nd4 36. Nxd4 cxd4 37. Nf5 Be6 38. Rfc1 Bxf5 39. gxf5 c5 40. Kf2 Rb2 41.
Qe2 Qxa2 42. f4 Kh6 43. fxg5+ fxg5 44. Kg2 a5 45. Kf3 a4 46. Kg4 {Now Black is
careless:} a3 $2 (46... Rxc2 47. Rxc2 Qb1 {leads to an easy win - JS}) 47. Qd2
$1 Rg8 {Black thinks he has covered the threat but White has a further trick.}
48. f6 $1 Kh7 $2 ({After the correct} 48... Rxc2 {White has} 49. f7 $1 Rxd2 50.
fxg8=Q Rg2+ 51. Kf5 Qf2+ 52. Ke6 {and White has perpetual check. The move
played should actually lose, but White misses his chance of getting the full
point. (JS)}) 49. Kf5 $2 (49. f7 $1 Rf8 50. Qxg5 (50. Kh5 Rxf7 51. Qxg5 Rg7 52.
Qf5+ Kg8 53. Rf1 {comes to much the same thing}) 50... Rxc2 51. Kh5 Rxf7 52.
Rg1 {is game over - Stockfish}) 49... Qb3 50. f7 Rf8 51. Qxg5 Rxf7+ 52. Ke6 Qb7
53. Qh5+ Kg8 54. Rg1+ Rg7 55. Qe8+ Kh7 56. Qh5+ {Sources: West Sussex Gazette
of 22.9.1904, 'Chess Tournaments and Matches 1904' - A J Gillam - The Chess
Player (1999), and the Big Database 2003. Some comments on the finish: London
Daily News, 28 Jul 1904} 1/2-1/2

