1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Be2 d6 5. d4 Be7 6. h3 h6 7. Be3 a6 8. a4 Nd7
9. Nd5 exd4 10. Nxd4 Nf6 11. Nc3 Nxd4 12. Qxd4 O-O 13. O-O Ne8 14. Nd5 Be6 15.
Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Bd3 c5 17. Qc3 c4 18. Be2 d5 19. exd5 Bxd5 20. Rad1 Nf6 21. Rfe1
Rfe8 22. Qa5 {[%mdl 32768] [diag]} b5 23. axb5 Qb7 $5 {An enterprising idea
which ultimately pays off.} 24. Bf1 ({Engines find} 24. Qc3 $1 {when} Bxg2 {
runs into} 25. Bxh6 {and the silicon tell us the complications favour White. A
human would need nerves of steel to go down that line.}) 24... axb5 25. Qc3 Re6
26. f3 ({Once again the silicon prescribes} 26. Bxh6 $1 {and this time it
looks not beyond human powers of calculation after, say,} b4 27. Qg3 Nh5 28.
Qg5 {, etc.}) 26... Bc6 27. Bf2 Nd5 28. Qd4 Rg6 29. Re5 $2 (29. Kh1 {,
breaking the pin on the g-file, was called for here.}) 29... Nb4 $1 {Now both
the f-pawn and c-pawn are under threat.} 30. Qd2 $2 (30. Qh4 $1 {is a handy
defence. If now} Bxf3 $2 {White punishes Black with} 31. Rd8+ Rxd8 32. Qxd8+
Kh7 33. Re8 {and White's attack on the opposing king proves the more potent, e.
g.} Rg5 34. h4 $1 Rg4 35. h5 {and it's all over.}) 30... Bxf3 $1 31. Qxb4 Bxd1
32. Rxb5 Qc7 33. Rb7 Qe5 (33... Qf4 {is better.}) 34. Bxc4 ({Unsurprisingly,
in a sharp position the computer finds a lot of good chances that the players
missed. Here} 34. Rxf7 $1 {might well have saved White:} Be2 $5 (34... Kxf7 $2
35. Qb7+ Kf6 36. Qxa8 {is good for White}) 35. Qe7 {, etc.}) 34... Rf6 ({
Here the computer dishes up} 34... Bf3 $1 {which clinches the win for Black.})
35. Bd3 $2 {[diag]} (35. Bxf7+ $1 {again, when} Rxf7 36. Rxf7 {where White has
what chances are going.}) 35... Bxc2 $1 {Finally one of the players finds a
decisive tactic. The threat of Ra1+ is devastating.} 36. Bf1 Qf5 37. Qe1 Ra1 {
Sources: London Daily News, 4 Aug 1904; BCM 1904, p353; annotations by JS,
CHESS, Aug 2017, p37; The Daily News (London), 4 Aug 1904 and 13 Apr 1912,
Times Literary Supplement, 26 Aug 1904, Illustrated London News, 10 Sept 1904,
'Chess Tournaments and Matches 1904' - A J Gillam - The Chess Player (1999),
and the Big Database 2003.} (37... Ra1 38. Qxa1 Qxf2+ 39. Kh2 Qxf1 {leaves
Black a clear piece up.}) 0-1

