{[%mdl 32768]} 1. e4 e5 2. d4 {Mieses remained faithful to the romantic
openings of the 19th century, although ironically he outlived most of his
hypermodern rivals. His handling of the opening in this game does not impress.}
exd4 3. c3 d5 4. exd5 c6 5. cxd4 (5. Nf3 {may be better. After the text the
symmetrical position doesn't seem to offer White much of an opening edge.})
5... cxd5 6. Nc3 Nc6 7. Nf3 Nf6 8. Bd3 Be7 9. Ne5 O-O 10. O-O Ne8 {This, and
Black's subsequent handling of the kingside defence, looks a bit odd.} 11. Be3
g6 12. f4 Nxe5 13. fxe5 Be6 14. Ne2 ({Losing time. A more direct approach such
as} 14. Bh6 Ng7 15. Qd2 Qb6 16. Qf2 f6 17. Qe3 Rf7 {leads to an edge for White,
though perhaps Black can survive.}) 14... Ng7 15. Bh6 Bg5 16. Bxg5 Qxg5 17. Nf4
Rac8 18. Rf2 $6 (18. Qf3 {, attacking the d-pawn and restraining the f6 push,
looks better.}) 18... f6 $1 19. Qb3 $6 ({The computer indicates something like
} 19. Qe2 {or 19.Qe1 to shore up the e5-pawn but White wants to mix things up
a bit against one of the tournament back-markers.}) 19... fxe5 20. Nxe6 Nxe6
21. Rxf8+ ({The immediate} 21. Qxd5 $5 {looks risky but may work out better
after} Rc1+ 22. Rxc1 Qxc1+ 23. Rf1 Qe3+ 24. Kh1 Rxf1+ 25. Bxf1 exd4 26. g3 {
, when White should regain his sacrificed and get good value from his bishop
on the light squares.}) 21... Kxf8 22. Rf1+ Kg7 23. Qxb7+ Rc7 {[diag]} 24. Qxd5
$2 ({A serious miscalculation. Instead} 24. Qa6 Qe3+ 25. Kh1 exd4 {leaves
White worse but not yet lost.}) 24... Qe3+ 25. Kh1 Qxd3 26. Qxe5+ Kh6 27. Re1 {
White must have relied on this move to save himself (or even to win) but there
is a fatal flaw.} Rf7 $1 ({The back rank mate threat enables Black to retain
his extra piece. Not} 27... Qc4 $2 28. b3 $1 {and White will be able to play
Qxe6 to draw the game.} (28. Qxe6 $4 Qxe6 29. Rxe6 Rc1+ {mates})) 28. h4 Rf1+
29. Rxf1 Qxf1+ 30. Kh2 Qf4+ 31. Qxf4+ Nxf4 32. b4 Kg7 33. b5 Ne2 34. a4 Nxd4
35. g4 h6 36. Kg2 Kf6 37. Kf2 Ke5 38. Ke3 h5 39. g5 Kd5 40. Kd3 Kc5 41. Ke3
Nf5+ {Source: Mega/Big Database; annotations by JS, CHESS, Aug 2017, p36} 0-1

