{[%mdl 32768]} 1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 Bd6 $6 {The Manchester
Guardian (23 Aug 1907) referred to "out of the way" defence in this game and
they were perhaps referring to this dubious move.} 5. Bd3 O-O 6. f4 b6 7. Nf3
Bb7 8. Bd2 h6 9. Qe2 Ne4 10. O-O-O {[diag] Shoosmith's published reputation as
a player was for being an attacking player, and there is some evidence of it
here. His plan is direct: to castle queenside and then conduct an offensive
against Black's king.} a5 $6 ({Having already lost time with 4...Bd6, he
cannot really afford this. Perhaps} 10... Nxd2 11. Qxd2 Bb4 12. Rhg1 d6 {
is some sort of a defensive set-up.}) 11. Rhg1 Bb4 12. g4 {Black's position is
already critical, arguably beyond the point of no return since he has no
viable counterplay.} Qe7 13. Bxe4 fxe4 14. Ne5 Qe8 15. g5 d6 {[diag]} 16. gxh6
$1 {Not a particularly difficult sacrifice to calculate but as the culmination
of some brisk play by White, deserves an exclamation mark.} dxe5 17. Rxg7+ Kh8
18. Qg4 Qf7 ({One wonders why Blackburne prolongs his suffering. Perhaps he
didn't want to see headlines announcing how he had "lost in fewer than 20
moves".} 18... Rg8 19. Rg1 Nd7 20. h7 {was crushing.}) 19. Rxf7 Rxf7 20. Qg6
Re7 21. Qf6+ Kh7 22. Rg1 Nd7 23. Rg7+ Kh8 24. Qg6 {Sources: Yorkshire Post and
Leeds Intelligencer, 23 Aug 1907; Sheffield Daily Telegraph, 23 Aug 1907 
(mistitled as Shoosmith-Mackenzie); Birmingham Daily Post, 23 Aug 1907;
Belfast Newsletter, 23 Aug 1907; London Daily News, 29 Aug 1907; annotations
by JS, CHESS, Aug 2017, p36} 1-0

