1. d4 c5 2. e3 e6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Bd3 b6 5. O-O Bb7 6. Nbd2 Nc6 7. b3 Rc8 8. a3
cxd4 9. exd4 Be7 10. Bb2 O-O 11. Re1 Nd5 12. c4 Nf4 13. Bf1 d5 14. Rc1 Bf6 15.
c5 bxc5 16. Rxc5 Be7 17. Rc2 Qb6 18. g3 Ng6 19. b4 Nb8 20. Nb3 Bc6 21. Qc1 Bd7
22. Nc5 Bc6 23. Qd2 Bb5 24. Bxb5 Qxb5 25. a4 Qb6 26. Rec1 Rfd8 27. Ba3 Nc6 28.
b5 Na5 29. Bb4 Nc4 30. Qe1 Qb8 31. Nd2 Nxd2 32. Bxd2 Bxc5 33. Rxc5 Rxc5 34.
Rxc5 e5 35. Ba5 Re8 36. Bc7 exd4 37. Qd1 Qc8 38. Qxd4 Qh3 39. Rc1 $2 ({Having
thoroughly outplayed his illustrious opponent to here, Shoosmith starts to
waver:} 39. Ba5 $1 {defends against the mate in one and also gets ready to
threaten a mate of his own with Bc3, plus occupying the seventh rank with Rc7.
Black has no good reply.}) 39... Qe6 $2 ({Blackburne misses a cheapo chance.
Instead,} 39... Qd7 $1 {, hoping for} 40. Qxa7 $2 Nf4 $1 {when White has
nothing better than to allow the perpetual check after} 41. gxf4 Qg4+ {, etc.})
40. Kg2 $2 ({After Black's poor last move, there seems to be no good reason
why White shouldn't now play} 40. Qxa7 {. Putting his king on g2 makes little
sense - JS}) 40... h5 41. h4 Qf5 42. Rc5 Re2 43. Rxd5 $2 ({Black has a
comfortable draw after this. At first sight the plan of} 43. Bb8 Ne7 44. Bxa7 {
seems good but in fact} Re1 $1 {, followed by Qb1, should be enough to draw
for Black.}) ({However, White could instead play} 43. Bd6 {, keeping up the
pressure.}) 43... Rxf2+ 44. Qxf2 Qxd5+ 45. Kh2 Qc4 46. Qxa7 Qe2+ 47. Kg1 Qe1+
48. Kg2 Qe4+ 49. Kf1 Qd3+ 50. Ke1 Qc3+ 51. Ke2 Qc2+ 52. Ke3 Qc3+ 53. Ke2 Qc2+ {
Source: BCM 1908, p446} 1/2-1/2

